<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="
          http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
          https://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.23.xsd">

    <!--
      "Zapisnik povezanog stola je javan" — BLOK-LINK.md §6.2.

      One nullable column on `match_score_links`: which blok SERIES (the
      client's own `sessionId`, the same string `blok_sessions.session_id`
      holds) is being played at the linked table.

      Why it is here at all:

        * Linking a blok to a table IS the player's consent to publish that
          scorepad — the organiser and anyone holding the link must be able
          to open it, during and after the tournament. The server therefore
          mints the series' `blok_sessions.share_token` (BLOK-HISTORY.md
          §5.2) the first time a score is written through an APPROVED link,
          and it can only find that row if the link says which series it is.

        * It is NOT a foreign key to `blok_sessions`. The blok links the
          table before it has ever uploaded the series (upload needs the
          player to save; linking does not), so the row this string names
          routinely does not exist yet — and may never, if the player never
          saves. A FK would refuse exactly the ordinary case. The pair
          (`requested_by_uid`, `session_id`) is what `blok_sessions`'
          `uq_blok_sessions_user_session` is keyed on, so the lookup is an
          indexed read anyway, and a series can only ever be resolved for
          the uid that asked for the link — which is the consent boundary.

        * NULLABLE, with no backfill: links made before this column existed
          have no series to name, and a link whose client is older than
          §6.2 never sends one. Both simply carry no shareable record. The
          service also accepts the id later, on the first score push, so a
          link that predates the client that knows its series can still
          adopt one.

      varchar(64) matches `blok_sessions.session_id` exactly; the service
      treats anything longer or blank as absent rather than failing the score
      write, since §6.2 makes the token a consequence of the score and never
      a condition for it.
    -->

    <changeSet id="2026-09-08-blok-link-session" author="mrodek">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <columnExists tableName="match_score_links" columnName="session_id"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>

        <addColumn tableName="match_score_links">
            <!-- The client's series id, or NULL when this link names no series.
                 Same width and meaning as blok_sessions.session_id. -->
            <column name="session_id" type="varchar(64)"/>
        </addColumn>

        <rollback>
            <dropColumn tableName="match_score_links" columnName="session_id"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
